  assistant ali who is traveling today  quickly noticed from all of the buzz  around this day that this is one of the  university church's most important feast  days we have claimed it and we put a lot  of energy and love into this day what  most people remember about world  communion sunday i would guess is that  we offer many different kinds of bread  during our holy communion or bari from  iran thank you mourad gingerbread from  carol Conroy's kitchen rice cakes from  southeast asia brought by shell nine  different kinds of bread from all around  the world will be for us today christ's  body and blood what I hope we remember  besides how wonderful the cornbread or  the non tastes this morning is that  Christians around the world celebrate  this sacrament using the staple breads  of their local communities maybe today's  feast with nine breads will linger with  us a little longer than just today maybe  this memory will come with us back to  church two weeks from now for Holy  Communion again when we take a piece of  our wonderful rich you see why communion  bread a recipe Ian brought and remember  that this is about one kind of bread  that Christians around the world used to  remember the meal on the night in which  Jesus was betrayed we just sang those  wonderful words from Psalm 133 behold  how good  and how pleasant it is for kindred to  dwell together in unity but let's be  honest sharing bread from another  culture is not the definition of unity  though it is fun singing songs from  Haiti or Zimbabwe this morning does not  fully unite us with those living amid  earthquake rubble or in a country  ravaged by hiv/aids sure in our  communion bread in our global songs we  are on our way to unity but we are not  there yet when Christians in Iran are  imprisoned because of their faith yet  many of us take our religious liberties  for granted we are not there yet when  Christians with in denominations battle  over doctrine and those tensions become  the focus instead of Christ call to work  for justice we are not there yet when  Christians define who is a real believer  and who's a fake and forget that in  Christ we are one family we are not  there yet how good and how pleasant it  is when kindred when Christian sisters  and brothers on every continent live  together in unity and we are not there  yet world communion sunday should not  just be a celebration of the gifts of  many cultures it is also a day that  calls us to humility patience repentance  deep listening Paul's letter to the  Ephesians which we heard in Farsi this  morning and read in English has an  urgent tone I beg you to lead a life  worthy of the calling to which you were  called central concern for Paul or one  of his disciples that may have penned  this letter in his name we don't know a  central concern is unity Paul calls his  church members of the household of God  and he continues to use the imagery of  the body knit together by every ligament  to describe the church  Paul begs us to live a life worthy of  the calling to which we have been called  unity are we worthy of this calling how  are we doing when it comes to global  global and local Christian unity a  couple of years ago in his New York  Times column Peter Steinfeld's asked has  the movement for unity among Christians  gone into a coma out our Christians  worthy of the calling to unite with each  other and Christ that calling so urgent  for Paul maybe were not so worthy but if  the church gave up every time it wasn't  living up to the calling of God we would  be in an even bigger mess than we  already are if as individuals we gave up  every time we weren't following Christ  examples perfectly we would also be in  trouble and so we trust that our God is  a God of second chances of third and  fourth and fifth and ten thousandth  chances so what might we do to be worthy  of that calling to live in unity with  other Christians and now I realize I've  interrupted the Apostle Paul sentence  earlier so 